---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  Chips,
  Copy,
  Eyebrow,
  Grid,
  Head,
  Heading,
  HeroCTAs,
  HeroStack,
  Lede,
  Links,
  P,
  PageHero,
  RuledCard,
  Sec,
  StackHead,
  StackNote,
  StackPanel,
  Two,
} from '../../components/ds';
import { INNER_CTAS } from '../../data/site';

const CATALOGS = ['OpenMetadata', 'DataHub', 'Collibra', 'Atlan', 'Apache Atlas', 'OpenLineage'];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Feeds the catalog you already bought — Provisa"
  description="One registration publishes to OpenMetadata, DataHub, Collibra, Atlan, Apache Atlas, and OpenLineage — with lineage stitched on business identity and every governed value addressable."
>
  <PageHero crumb="Fits your stack">
    <Eyebrow>Fits your stack</Eyebrow>
    <Heading level="page">It feeds the catalog you already bought.</Heading>
    <Lede>
      Provisa is not another place your metadata goes to die. The model that governs your queries publishes outward to the tools your organization
      already runs — and addresses every governed value it holds.
    </Lede>
    <HeroCTAs primary={INNER_CTAS.primary} secondary={INNER_CTAS.secondary} />
    <HeroStack slot="aside" label="One registration, six catalog destinations">
      <StackHead>ONE REGISTRATION · tables, columns, tags, classifications, lineage</StackHead>
      <StackPanel label="SIX DESTINATIONS">
        <Chips items={CATALOGS} />
      </StackPanel>
      <StackNote label="PUBLISHED">From the admin console, or on a cron</StackNote>
    </HeroStack>
  </PageHero>

  <Sec band="white" labelledBy="publishes-title">
    <Two>
      <Head>
        <Heading level="section-sm" id="publishes-title">Publishes to your catalog</Heading>
      </Head>
      <Copy>
        <Heading level="lede">
          One registration, six destinations: <strong>OpenMetadata</strong>, <strong>DataHub</strong>, <strong>Collibra</strong>, <strong>Atlan</strong>,
          <strong>Apache Atlas</strong>, and <strong>OpenLineage</strong>. Tables, columns, tags, classifications, and lineage flow out as first-class
          catalog assets, from the admin console or from <code>provisa metadata export</code> on a cron.
        </Heading>
        <P>
          The point is not that Provisa has a catalog. It is that the metadata is a byproduct of the thing that already has to be correct — the model
          that executes and governs your queries — rather than a parallel description maintained by hand and drifting from the moment it is written.
        </P>
      </Copy>
    </Two>
  </Sec>

  <Sec label="Lineage and data quality">
    <Grid min={380} gap={48} gapX={64}>
      <RuledCard rule="cobalt" weight={4} titleSize={28} as="h2" title="Lineage that survives re-platforming">
        <P>
          Assets are stitched on <strong>business identity</strong>, not physical location. Move a table from Oracle to Postgres and its catalog entry,
          lineage, and history follow the business entity instead of shattering into a new asset with no past. A migration doesn't reset your
          catalog to zero.
        </P>
      </RuledCard>
      <RuledCard rule="forest" weight={4} titleSize={28} as="h2" title="Data quality, without a second engine">
        <P>
          Point <strong>Soda</strong> at a governed table and register the contract as a source. You get scan history, a quality scorecard, and
          lineage from each failing check back to the table it guards — with scans triggered by <em>change</em> rather than by a cron guess. Provisa
          ships no data-quality engine and doesn't ask you to retire yours.
        </P>
      </RuledCard>
    </Grid>
  </Sec>

  <Sec band="white" labelledBy="address-title">
    <Two>
      <Head>
        <Heading level="section-sm" id="address-title">Every value has an address</Heading>
      </Head>
      <Copy>
        <P>
          A governed value is addressable as <code>provisa://&lt;org&gt;/&lt;domain&gt;/&lt;table&gt;/pk=&lt;id&gt;/&lt;column&gt;</code> and resolves over
          REST or MCP — optionally <em>as of</em> a point in time. Exported documents, lineage nodes, audit records, and AI-agent citations carry links
          that resolve back to the live governed value, under the same policy as any other read.
        </P>
        <P>
          Only semantic addresses dereference. Physical URIs are refused, so a citation can never leak a location — which matters most when the thing
          doing the citing is an agent whose output you cannot review line by line.
        </P>
        <Links
          links={[
            { label: 'The governance underneath', href: '/why/governance' },
            { label: 'Why your semantics stay yours', href: '/why/no-lock-in' },
          ]}
        />
      </Copy>
    </Two>
  </Sec>
</BaseLayout>
